WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.542 --> 00:00:01.375
Tyson Fury

00:00:01.375 --> 00:00:04.082
his view, he says for
the best place for women,

00:00:04.082 --> 00:00:05.360
is being on their backs or in the kitchen.

00:00:05.360 --> 00:00:06.610
I'm glad he's not my client.

00:00:06.610 --> 00:00:08.584
When it's spilled over, go on.

00:00:08.584 --> 00:00:09.692
What's a bad moment?

00:00:09.692 --> 00:00:10.525
I mean, the famous one was

00:00:10.525 --> 00:00:11.513
Derek Chisora and Dillian Whyte,

00:00:11.513 --> 00:00:13.254
where he picked up the table,

00:00:13.254 --> 00:00:15.413
the press conference table and threw it.

00:00:15.413 --> 00:00:16.809
It was aimed at Dillian Whyte,

00:00:16.809 --> 00:00:17.670
but it actually went on myself.

00:00:17.670 --> 00:00:20.120
Andy Murray, turned down one
and a half million pounds

00:00:20.120 --> 00:00:22.781
to play in Saudi, because
of human rights concerns,

00:00:22.781 --> 00:00:23.614
it does happen. I suppose,

00:00:23.614 --> 00:00:24.940
there are individuals who take that,

00:00:24.940 --> 00:00:26.094
Yeah, but it was a lot
more money than that,

00:00:26.094 --> 00:00:27.850
and maybe if they offered Andy
Murray more he would've gone.

00:00:27.850 --> 00:00:30.300
That was the boxing promoter, Eddie Hearn.

00:00:30.300 --> 00:00:31.989
In this conversation,
you are going to hear

00:00:31.989 --> 00:00:35.950
about why he didn't go to
court for Anthony Joshua

00:00:35.950 --> 00:00:38.340
and why men still want to punch each other

00:00:38.340 --> 00:00:39.173
in the face.

00:00:54.750 --> 00:00:58.910
It's both the same and very different,

00:00:58.910 --> 00:01:01.581
either way, promoters are
more powerful than ever.

00:01:01.581 --> 00:01:04.003
and the stakes are just as high.

00:01:06.220 --> 00:01:08.640
Hello and welcome, my guest this week

00:01:08.640 --> 00:01:10.580
is promoter, entrepreneur,
and arguably one

00:01:10.580 --> 00:01:14.490
of the most powerful people
in boxing, Eddie Hearn.

00:01:14.490 --> 00:01:16.709
His company, Matchroom
Sport has been credited

00:01:16.709 --> 00:01:19.310
with taking boxing to another level.

00:01:19.310 --> 00:01:21.300
And his partnership with Anthony Joshua,

00:01:21.300 --> 00:01:23.120
has got him into a few scrapes of his own.

00:01:23.120 --> 00:01:25.800
Luckily just verbal, welcome Eddie.

00:01:25.800 --> 00:01:26.633
Thank you.

00:01:26.633 --> 00:01:28.990
Thanks for being here, and
I wanted to start by asking,

00:01:28.990 --> 00:01:31.149
why do two people, do you think,

00:01:31.149 --> 00:01:34.900
want to still punch each
other in the face, in 2022?

00:01:34.900 --> 00:01:36.210
It is bizarre, isn't it?

00:01:36.210 --> 00:01:38.773
And, there's kind of no excuse for boxing.

00:01:38.773 --> 00:01:41.050
It's very difficult to explain to people,

00:01:41.050 --> 00:01:44.560
and you know, the, the
argument will always come up.

00:01:44.560 --> 00:01:46.520
If there is an injury in the sport,

00:01:46.520 --> 00:01:49.470
specifically, which
has become a lot safer,

00:01:49.470 --> 00:01:52.300
how does this exist, in
this, this modern day

00:01:52.300 --> 00:01:53.639
world that we live in?

00:01:53.639 --> 00:01:56.620
It's historic, there's so
many fundamentals to boxing,

00:01:56.620 --> 00:01:58.100
which we'll probably
come to about actually

00:01:58.100 --> 00:02:01.280
what the sport can teach
the younger generation.

00:02:01.280 --> 00:02:02.541
But none of it really makes sense.

00:02:02.541 --> 00:02:06.600
And it's the most compelling
sport, for me, in the world,

00:02:06.600 --> 00:02:07.650
the most addictive sport.

00:02:07.650 --> 00:02:09.980
Because, when you explain it to people,

00:02:09.980 --> 00:02:13.390
it is two men and now women as well,

00:02:13.390 --> 00:02:17.210
going into a 20 by 20
foot ring to have a fight

00:02:17.210 --> 00:02:20.376
with each other in front of the world.

00:02:20.376 --> 00:02:22.610
But what's going on between those two

00:02:22.610 --> 00:02:24.100
people in your experience?

00:02:24.100 --> 00:02:25.710
Because sometimes you will
have known both of them,

00:02:25.710 --> 00:02:27.150
when you are promoting.

00:02:27.150 --> 00:02:29.971
Do they actually want
to hurt the individual

00:02:29.971 --> 00:02:32.709
or is it about what they're doing?

00:02:32.709 --> 00:02:34.960
I think that, you know,
it's about winning.

00:02:34.960 --> 00:02:36.590
It's a sport at the end of the day.

00:02:36.590 --> 00:02:39.050
I don't think, sometimes people don't get

00:02:39.050 --> 00:02:39.883
on with the opponent.

00:02:39.883 --> 00:02:42.120
You know there's a,
there's a lot of things

00:02:42.120 --> 00:02:43.181
I've heard,

00:02:43.181 --> 00:02:44.650
Yeah, there's a lot of things
said backwards and forwards,

00:02:44.650 --> 00:02:46.260
but generally, it's about, it's about

00:02:46.260 --> 00:02:49.390
winning the fight tactically,
going in there and, and,

00:02:49.390 --> 00:02:51.770
you know, executing a performance

00:02:51.770 --> 00:02:53.909
that you've been working
on for weeks, months,

00:02:53.909 --> 00:02:56.920
sometimes years, a fighter's
career reaches that moment.

00:02:56.920 --> 00:02:59.270
But again, there are, these people,

00:02:59.270 --> 00:03:02.150
they're not always normal people.

00:03:02.150 --> 00:03:05.740
They can't be normal actually
to, to, do what they do

00:03:05.740 --> 00:03:06.960
and to fight at that level.

00:03:06.960 --> 00:03:09.800
So, you have to be a little
bit careful from my position.

00:03:09.800 --> 00:03:12.000
You've gotta sell the
fight, hype the fight.

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:13.510
Sometimes it spills over.

00:03:13.510 --> 00:03:16.030
I've been been in the middle
of that quite a few times.

00:03:16.030 --> 00:03:18.170
But generally that sort of back and forth,

00:03:18.170 --> 00:03:20.260
that animosity, that tension, and energy

00:03:20.260 --> 00:03:24.170
between two fighters, is
ultimately what sells.

00:03:24.170 --> 00:03:26.900
You know, it's what's gonna
create narrative and interest

00:03:26.900 --> 00:03:29.630
to the public and to, to fight fans.

00:03:29.630 --> 00:03:30.627
When it's spilled over, go on.

00:03:30.627 --> 00:03:32.501
What's a bad moment?

00:03:32.501 --> 00:03:33.587
I mean, the famous one

00:03:33.587 --> 00:03:34.420
was Derek Chisora and Dillian Whyte,

00:03:34.420 --> 00:03:36.050
where he picked up the table,

00:03:36.050 --> 00:03:38.280
the press conference table, and threw it,

00:03:38.280 --> 00:03:39.150
It was aimed at Dillian Whyte.

00:03:39.150 --> 00:03:41.670
But it actually went on myself,
and Adam Smith, from Sky

00:03:41.670 --> 00:03:44.390
and some great photos of that,
and stuff like that, but,

00:03:44.390 --> 00:03:45.500
Were you scared?

00:03:45.500 --> 00:03:46.643
Not really, I've been around boxing

00:03:46.643 --> 00:03:48.040
since I was seven years old.

00:03:48.040 --> 00:03:49.960
Yeah, but a table being thrown at you!

00:03:49.960 --> 00:03:51.249
Had that happened before?

00:03:51.249 --> 00:03:52.829
It was sort of, you know, blocked it,

00:03:52.829 --> 00:03:53.662
but that way, that was a new one.

00:03:53.662 --> 00:03:55.800
But you know, I've been
around because of, of,

00:03:55.800 --> 00:03:57.640
you know, my dad being a promoter as well.

00:03:57.640 --> 00:03:59.950
I've been around boxing
since I was eight years old.

00:03:59.950 --> 00:04:03.440
I've been in the middle of
fights in, at press conferences.

00:04:03.440 --> 00:04:06.130
I've been in the middle of
fighters getting injured,

00:04:06.130 --> 00:04:08.473
or crying backstage,
or cheering backstage,

00:04:08.473 --> 00:04:10.646
or you know, every kind of emotion

00:04:10.646 --> 00:04:11.980
that you see in a sport.

00:04:11.980 --> 00:04:14.420
And it's incredibly
addictive because it is,

00:04:14.420 --> 00:04:16.040
it's such a compelling sport.

00:04:16.040 --> 00:04:19.070
And like I said earlier,
it's just, it's not normal.

00:04:19.070 --> 00:04:20.110
Are you any good at fighting?

00:04:20.110 --> 00:04:22.480
I had a couple of
amateur fights, terrible.

00:04:22.480 --> 00:04:23.313
Are you tough now?

00:04:23.313 --> 00:04:25.610
I'm not tough, I mean, I
was, I'm a silver spoon kid.

00:04:25.610 --> 00:04:26.980
Aren't I, as my dad always called me,

00:04:26.980 --> 00:04:28.949
but you know I, I grew up.

00:04:28.949 --> 00:04:32.030
You know, my weekends were hanging around

00:04:32.030 --> 00:04:35.340
with Naseem Hamed, or Chris
Eubank, or Frank Bruno.

00:04:35.340 --> 00:04:39.200
So I thought I could
fight, but you, you don't

00:04:39.200 --> 00:04:41.580
become really a fighter when you come

00:04:41.580 --> 00:04:42.850
from my kind of background.

00:04:42.850 --> 00:04:44.349
You don't think no.

00:04:44.349 --> 00:04:46.020
No, very rarely will you find someone

00:04:46.020 --> 00:04:49.470
who's come, come from a, you
know, a privileged background,

00:04:49.470 --> 00:04:50.620
really, which I would
say I have, you know,

00:04:50.620 --> 00:04:52.810
my dad's from Dagenham, and

00:04:52.810 --> 00:04:56.100
you know, east London, but
he, he was successful and I,

00:04:56.100 --> 00:04:58.226
I was very lucky, but it's
a different kind of life

00:04:58.226 --> 00:05:00.499
you have to live as a
fighter and to come through

00:05:00.499 --> 00:05:02.920
the ranks like these young men and women,

00:05:02.920 --> 00:05:05.310
do you have to be made of tougher stuff.

00:05:05.310 --> 00:05:08.310
And very rarely will you see
someone come from that kind of

00:05:08.310 --> 00:05:11.004
background um, to go on
and make it in the sport.

00:05:11.004 --> 00:05:11.971
And, and again, that's,

00:05:11.971 --> 00:05:15.560
what's compelling as well
to be part of that journey.

00:05:15.560 --> 00:05:16.393
My dad always said to me,

00:05:16.393 --> 00:05:19.740
if you can get 1% of the
adrenaline that these fighters get,

00:05:19.740 --> 00:05:22.760
when they walk out to fight,
you are a very lucky man.

00:05:22.760 --> 00:05:25.700
And I feel like sometimes
I get 80% because you know,

00:05:25.700 --> 00:05:27.680
the buzz is what we live for.

00:05:27.680 --> 00:05:28.950
Also the money must be pretty good,

00:05:28.950 --> 00:05:31.908
a billion dollar TV deal to
stream all of your fights.

00:05:31.908 --> 00:05:34.540
How do you explain your
job apart from, you know,

00:05:34.540 --> 00:05:37.760
being in the action?
What do you actually do?

00:05:37.760 --> 00:05:39.610
Well, I, I guess I put it together.

00:05:41.080 --> 00:05:45.310
I kind of like, when we started,
I started promoting boxing,

00:05:45.310 --> 00:05:47.598
which was probably about
12 or 13 years ago.

00:05:47.598 --> 00:05:51.510
I never went in with the
attention to kind of build my

00:05:51.510 --> 00:05:55.170
profile to a level where I'm
part of the show as well.

00:05:55.170 --> 00:05:58.010
And I should have, because
that was the right thing to do,

00:05:58.010 --> 00:06:00.955
but it just emerged over
the years as we went.

00:06:00.955 --> 00:06:03.330
But I'm like the, you know, the,

00:06:03.330 --> 00:06:05.680
the guy who puts the
circus together, you know,

00:06:05.680 --> 00:06:08.500
it's my job to go out,
to build the fights,

00:06:08.500 --> 00:06:10.530
to create the narrative,
to sell the story,

00:06:10.530 --> 00:06:14.300
to get the public and fight
fans invested in the event and

00:06:14.300 --> 00:06:16.330
fights. And that's what
we do at Matchroom.

00:06:16.330 --> 00:06:18.820
And we've gone, of course,
from, you know, being a, a,

00:06:18.820 --> 00:06:23.290
a big UK promoter to being a
global powerhouse in boxing,

00:06:23.290 --> 00:06:24.960
That streaming deal is with DAZN,

00:06:24.960 --> 00:06:28.559
our TV partners and streaming
global streaming giant.

00:06:28.559 --> 00:06:31.640
And that's taken us into
the U.S. with a big deal,

00:06:31.640 --> 00:06:34.410
taking us into key
markets like Italy, Spain,

00:06:34.410 --> 00:06:36.729
Australia, Mexico and, and,

00:06:36.729 --> 00:06:39.100
We keep on expanding around the world.

00:06:39.100 --> 00:06:42.570
And that's the plan for us is
to dominate boxing globally

00:06:43.540 --> 00:06:44.600
with a different feel,

00:06:44.600 --> 00:06:47.430
with a different kind of
professional touch to the live

00:06:47.430 --> 00:06:48.263
event experience

00:06:48.263 --> 00:06:52.423
both in the arena and for
viewers watching on the platform

00:06:52.423 --> 00:06:54.650
So putting on that show,
getting it to people,

00:06:54.650 --> 00:06:56.850
putting the business side
of it together. But as you,

00:06:56.850 --> 00:06:59.000
you mentioned there about
taking a meeting, you know,

00:06:59.000 --> 00:07:00.890
how does, for instance, Anthony Joshua,

00:07:00.890 --> 00:07:02.650
how does that relationship start? Do you,

00:07:02.650 --> 00:07:04.510
do you give him a card? How do you meet?

00:07:04.510 --> 00:07:06.300
Yeah. Well with Anthony
Joshua, it's actually

00:07:06.300 --> 00:07:08.520
quite a funny story.
I'll tell you quickly.

00:07:08.520 --> 00:07:11.560
I received a letter once from
a gymnasium to say, look,

00:07:11.560 --> 00:07:15.190
you know, there's this really
promising young heavyweight,

00:07:15.190 --> 00:07:17.980
but he's got himself in some
trouble and he has to appear

00:07:17.980 --> 00:07:19.690
in court, and we would like

00:07:19.690 --> 00:07:22.938
you to come and talk about if this young

00:07:22.938 --> 00:07:27.461
man was as good as we think
he is, what could he achieve?

00:07:27.461 --> 00:07:31.220
Or what could the, you know,
the future hold for him.

00:07:31.220 --> 00:07:33.470
And I looked at the letter and thought,

00:07:33.470 --> 00:07:35.701
I don't even know the kid,
I'm not going to court.

00:07:35.701 --> 00:07:36.588
I'm not going to court.

00:07:36.588 --> 00:07:37.421
It went straight in the bin.

00:07:37.421 --> 00:07:39.120
And then I went up to the GB Institute.

00:07:39.120 --> 00:07:41.610
And I just heard this noise.
And it was a heavy bag.

00:07:41.610 --> 00:07:44.290
I know the noise well
just being moved around

00:07:44.290 --> 00:07:46.530
you know, on its pivot
and I turned around to

00:07:46.530 --> 00:07:47.860
Robert McCracken the coach and said,

00:07:47.860 --> 00:07:50.060
who's that? And he said,
that's Anthony Joshua,

00:07:50.060 --> 00:07:52.260
he's, this kid's special.

00:07:52.260 --> 00:07:55.230
And I thought, oh, no, that
was the name on the letter.

00:07:55.230 --> 00:07:57.340
I wish I would've responded. He went on,

00:07:57.340 --> 00:07:59.411
went into the Olympics
and you almost hoped they,

00:07:59.411 --> 00:08:02.211
don't win the Olympics because
then everybody's gonna be

00:08:02.211 --> 00:08:06.560
after his signature. Of course
he won gold in London 2012.

00:08:06.560 --> 00:08:10.140
In terms of British boxing,
probably world boxing as well,

00:08:10.140 --> 00:08:12.610
we are probably the first stop
that you would go to for a

00:08:12.610 --> 00:08:15.560
meeting, say, you know, when
you wanna meet other promoters,

00:08:15.560 --> 00:08:16.910
you'd, you'd come to Matchroom.

00:08:16.910 --> 00:08:18.850
So we had that meeting.

00:08:18.850 --> 00:08:21.130
Sometimes I've got the
benefit of being younger

00:08:21.130 --> 00:08:22.810
than the other promoters and probably

00:08:22.810 --> 00:08:25.220
get on better with the younger fighters.

00:08:25.220 --> 00:08:27.670
But, you know, with
Anthony, he was a, you know,

00:08:27.670 --> 00:08:31.030
he was a dream signing because
he won gold at the London

00:08:31.030 --> 00:08:33.450
2012 Olympics. He was a heavyweight.

00:08:33.450 --> 00:08:34.390
He was articulate.

00:08:34.390 --> 00:08:36.300
Did he forgive you for not going to court?

00:08:36.300 --> 00:08:39.068
I told him the story. Yeah,
he was probably, I couldn't,

00:08:39.068 --> 00:08:40.783
but I still couldn't believe
they asked me to do it.

00:08:41.680 --> 00:08:43.450
But um, he was, he's
very intelligent Anthony,

00:08:43.450 --> 00:08:45.490
and asked a lot of questions

00:08:45.490 --> 00:08:47.720
and I could see him sort
of look at me as if to say,

00:08:47.720 --> 00:08:50.220
I'm not sure I trust you guys.

00:08:50.220 --> 00:08:53.080
You know? And I said to him
at the meeting, I said, look,

00:08:53.080 --> 00:08:54.323
go and see everybody.

00:08:55.170 --> 00:08:57.280
And then take your time,
make your decision.

00:08:57.280 --> 00:08:59.810
Whereas everybody else said sign now.

00:08:59.810 --> 00:09:02.130
And he went away for about
three or four months.

00:09:02.130 --> 00:09:03.543
I never heard from him. I thought, oh,

00:09:03.543 --> 00:09:05.661
I shouldn't have said that.

00:09:05.661 --> 00:09:06.994
And then he came back
and he said, we wanna,

00:09:06.994 --> 00:09:07.830
we wanna sign with you
guys, which was, you know,

00:09:07.830 --> 00:09:09.700
a really big moment for our business.

00:09:09.700 --> 00:09:11.680
It is still a largely male sport.

00:09:11.680 --> 00:09:13.210
I know things are starting to change,

00:09:13.210 --> 00:09:17.710
but do you think in some ways
- boxing represents in some

00:09:17.710 --> 00:09:20.080
ways an outdated version
of masculinity or is it an

00:09:20.080 --> 00:09:21.730
important part of being a man?

00:09:21.730 --> 00:09:23.540
I think it's changing a
lot. You know, I think,

00:09:23.540 --> 00:09:26.780
I think the world is changing
and sport is changing in that

00:09:26.780 --> 00:09:29.349
respect as well. You know,
the openness across that,

00:09:29.349 --> 00:09:32.541
that area, whether you're
talking about mental health,

00:09:32.541 --> 00:09:35.400
you know, you see a lot
of fighters that struggle

00:09:35.400 --> 00:09:37.200
in that area, as you do
with a lot of sportsman.

00:09:37.200 --> 00:09:40.127
And I feel that people are, are,

00:09:40.127 --> 00:09:44.218
are willing to talk more
about many different talking

00:09:44.218 --> 00:09:47.800
points. Boxing has always
got the sort of, you know,

00:09:47.800 --> 00:09:49.950
the perception of being
a tough guy's sport.

00:09:49.950 --> 00:09:53.920
The influx of women's
boxing has been huge for us.

00:09:53.920 --> 00:09:55.750
And we've been the real trailblazer with,

00:09:55.750 --> 00:09:57.290
with the fighters to,

00:09:57.290 --> 00:09:59.410
to take that part of the sport forward.

00:09:59.410 --> 00:10:02.390
So I think it's changed a
lot over the years. I think,

00:10:02.390 --> 00:10:05.250
you know, back in the day,
if you want to say that

00:10:05.250 --> 00:10:08.291
that's that word, it was
definitely a sort of,

00:10:08.291 --> 00:10:09.891
you know, brawn and,

00:10:09.891 --> 00:10:12.401
It's still, it's still
like that though isn't it?

00:10:12.401 --> 00:10:13.560
It is. But I think it's
changing. You know,

00:10:13.560 --> 00:10:16.050
you've seen fashion brands
coming into the sport.

00:10:16.050 --> 00:10:17.710
You're seeing a younger generation with

00:10:17.710 --> 00:10:20.870
social media and content.
You're starting to understand

00:10:20.870 --> 00:10:22.900
the characters more,
people are opening up more.

00:10:22.900 --> 00:10:25.450
People are willing to talk
more people talk about their

00:10:25.450 --> 00:10:27.507
trials, tribulations, you
know, their struggles.

00:10:27.507 --> 00:10:28.429
They trash talk too.

00:10:28.429 --> 00:10:31.180
Trash talk's great, for
us, because you know,

00:10:31.180 --> 00:10:33.410
that's what, still, that's
what people wanna see.

00:10:33.410 --> 00:10:34.390
Yes. Sometimes I'll do a really,

00:10:34.390 --> 00:10:36.744
Yeah. They don't wanna see
somebody talking about their

00:10:36.744 --> 00:10:37.577
mental health, do they?

00:10:37.577 --> 00:10:38.552
No, but I think

00:10:38.552 --> 00:10:40.287
They, they wanna see
them being pugilistic.

00:10:40.287 --> 00:10:41.120
They wanna see all of that

00:10:41.120 --> 00:10:41.953
Exactly, but I've got,

00:10:41.953 --> 00:10:45.359
and I wonder, does that
perpetuate a stereotype of men

00:10:45.359 --> 00:10:46.192
that you are saying

00:10:46.192 --> 00:10:47.419
we're moving away from in other parts? I,

00:10:47.419 --> 00:10:49.734
and there might not be
anything wrong with that.

00:10:49.734 --> 00:10:51.509
I dunno if it is a stereotype,

00:10:51.509 --> 00:10:52.590
it's how a lot of men still are and feel.

00:10:52.590 --> 00:10:55.751
Yeah. But it's important
for us to tell stories.

00:10:55.751 --> 00:10:58.270
I mean, we are also, you
know, we are event promoters,

00:10:58.270 --> 00:11:00.760
but we're also content creators. You know,

00:11:00.760 --> 00:11:02.840
we run our own production
company. We know we,

00:11:02.840 --> 00:11:04.470
I like to produce content.

00:11:04.470 --> 00:11:07.491
I like to tell the story of a
fighter, and their struggle,

00:11:07.491 --> 00:11:08.580
and their life,

00:11:08.580 --> 00:11:12.809
because that's how you create
personalities and gain fans.

00:11:12.809 --> 00:11:15.940
And, you know, we need
fight fans to see that side,

00:11:15.940 --> 00:11:18.640
not just someone walking in
and put, going head to head

00:11:18.640 --> 00:11:21.171
with another guy and flexing his muscles.

00:11:21.171 --> 00:11:23.420
Tell the story. Every
individual in boxing,

00:11:23.420 --> 00:11:26.934
has a tremendous story, tremendous story.

00:11:26.934 --> 00:11:28.250
You know, I've got Lawrence Okolie

00:11:28.250 --> 00:11:29.740
the world cruiser weight champion.

00:11:29.740 --> 00:11:31.990
He was working at McDonald's in Waterloo.

00:11:31.990 --> 00:11:34.331
And one day he was flipping burgers.

00:11:34.331 --> 00:11:37.679
He was grossly overweight and
he saw Anthony Joshua win gold

00:11:37.679 --> 00:11:39.400
at London 2012.

00:11:39.400 --> 00:11:41.160
And he walked out of
McDonald's. He says, I'm gonna,

00:11:41.160 --> 00:11:43.220
I'm gonna take up boxing
and change my life.

00:11:43.220 --> 00:11:45.350
He's world cruiser weight
champion. You know, there,

00:11:45.350 --> 00:11:48.260
there are so many inspirational
stories in boxing.

00:11:48.260 --> 00:11:50.971
But there are some of the
most famous, Tyson Fury.

00:11:50.971 --> 00:11:53.349
And you look at what he
likes to say publicly.

00:11:53.349 --> 00:11:57.820
Would you say that is defined
by role model, manners?

00:11:57.820 --> 00:11:59.960
His view, he says for the
best place for women is being

00:11:59.960 --> 00:12:02.160
on their backs or in the kitchen.

00:12:02.160 --> 00:12:03.930
Mm. It's definitely not
something we support.

00:12:03.930 --> 00:12:06.620
And he's quite a unique
individual with that respect.

00:12:06.620 --> 00:12:07.550
But he's a big name.

00:12:07.550 --> 00:12:09.420
Yeah he is a big name and not everybody

00:12:09.420 --> 00:12:10.610
is everybody's role model

00:12:10.610 --> 00:12:11.765
Of course not

00:12:11.765 --> 00:12:13.765
but I suppose when you've
got those in the mix.

00:12:13.765 --> 00:12:15.047
Yeah. but sure. But for Tyson Fury

00:12:15.047 --> 00:12:16.711
away from those comments,

00:12:16.711 --> 00:12:19.902
now this is also a guy that
came back from pretty much

00:12:19.902 --> 00:12:21.690
obesity and, and, and
mental illness himself

00:12:21.690 --> 00:12:24.920
to a lot of people, he
is a role model now,

00:12:24.920 --> 00:12:25.753
Should he be?

00:12:25.753 --> 00:12:28.550
that, that, that's based on
the individual's perception.

00:12:28.550 --> 00:12:31.963
I think, it's not down to
me to say whether, you know,

00:12:31.963 --> 00:12:33.501
I look after myself and my family

00:12:33.501 --> 00:12:37.291
are they things that I
would want my children to,

00:12:37.291 --> 00:12:38.980
to look up to, of course not.

00:12:38.980 --> 00:12:40.580
I wouldn't even want 'em to see it,

00:12:40.580 --> 00:12:42.930
but the fighters that I know,

00:12:42.930 --> 00:12:46.150
Katie Taylor being the
best example in terms of my

00:12:46.150 --> 00:12:47.730
daughters, alright.

00:12:47.730 --> 00:12:51.289
There is no one that I would
like my kids to meet more and

00:12:51.289 --> 00:12:53.520
be around than Katie Taylor.

00:12:53.520 --> 00:12:54.930
So, is it very frustrating when there

00:12:54.930 --> 00:12:56.890
are those characters
in your world? I mean,

00:12:56.890 --> 00:12:59.330
he said he would celebrate
post March in 2020.

00:12:59.330 --> 00:13:01.256
I'm not holding you to account
for what Tyson Fury says.

00:13:01.256 --> 00:13:02.554
I'm glad he's not my client.

00:13:02.554 --> 00:13:04.410
but he did say he'd celebrate
with cocaine and hookers.

00:13:04.410 --> 00:13:06.300
You know, this, these
are the direct words.

00:13:06.300 --> 00:13:08.700
I think it's important to
share with, with our viewers,

00:13:08.700 --> 00:13:10.650
what I'm actually
talking about in the mix.

00:13:10.650 --> 00:13:13.590
I also think that regardless
of the fact that, yes,

00:13:13.590 --> 00:13:17.307
you're right. He is a big
name. It is in isolation.

00:13:17.307 --> 00:13:18.207
You're always gonna get them.

00:13:18.207 --> 00:13:20.531
You're always gonna get the
controversial characters.

00:13:20.531 --> 00:13:22.480
Sometimes, I think actually
he says things that

00:13:22.480 --> 00:13:24.330
he doesn't actually mean, but you know,

00:13:24.330 --> 00:13:25.163
that's another story.

00:13:25.163 --> 00:13:26.772
He says nice things about
you sometimes as well.

00:13:26.772 --> 00:13:28.630
Sometimes, sometimes not often.

00:13:28.630 --> 00:13:29.463
No.

00:13:29.463 --> 00:13:30.877
no. And, but again,

00:13:30.877 --> 00:13:32.030
He called you a bad businessman.

00:13:32.030 --> 00:13:33.014
Yeah, well yeah.

00:13:33.014 --> 00:13:33.847
Your last deal doesn't look so bad.

00:13:33.847 --> 00:13:36.179
Exactly. The record speaks for
itself. Bit like his boxing

00:13:36.179 --> 00:13:38.387
Do you, there we go, it's coming.

00:13:38.387 --> 00:13:39.930
Do you get frustrated though, by that?

00:13:39.930 --> 00:13:41.149
Does that bother you?

00:13:41.149 --> 00:13:41.982
No, It's all part of the game.

00:13:41.982 --> 00:13:43.940
And that's why when I
look at those comments,

00:13:43.940 --> 00:13:45.520
although they shouldn't be said,

00:13:45.520 --> 00:13:49.290
because he does have a position
as a role model in society

00:13:49.290 --> 00:13:52.290
They, they go over my head
because I know the man,

00:13:52.290 --> 00:13:53.294
you know, and,

00:13:53.294 --> 00:13:54.563
But they don't go over some peoples heads.

00:13:54.563 --> 00:13:56.570
No, I agree. But, but then, again,

00:13:56.570 --> 00:13:59.458
this is the real life and we
can't expect perfection from

00:13:59.458 --> 00:14:02.891
everybody, especially from
people that might not have

00:14:02.891 --> 00:14:04.560
been brought up in that way.

00:14:04.560 --> 00:14:06.080
And I don't mean that
disrespectfully for him,

00:14:06.080 --> 00:14:08.650
but you know, these
aren't normal individuals.

00:14:08.650 --> 00:14:10.710
Sometimes they say stupid things.

00:14:10.710 --> 00:14:13.040
Sometimes they do stupid
things, we all do.

00:14:13.040 --> 00:14:16.520
And sometimes people more so
than others. So, you know,

00:14:16.520 --> 00:14:19.580
in that respect, you can
never condone those comments.

00:14:19.580 --> 00:14:24.580
But, you know, again, I, I go
back to boxing, the community,

00:14:24.750 --> 00:14:27.340
what it does for people.
And I know, you know, again,

00:14:27.340 --> 00:14:29.070
going back to my kids,

00:14:29.070 --> 00:14:33.850
there is no place I'd rather
them be than in a boxing gym

00:14:33.850 --> 00:14:36.290
actually. And as for someone
who's got two daughters,

00:14:36.290 --> 00:14:39.130
I, I never thought I'd say
that until I started to get

00:14:39.130 --> 00:14:42.310
involved with women's boxing
and what Katie Taylor-

00:14:42.310 --> 00:14:45.500
What Katie Taylor's taught me.
What, how she's inspired me.

00:14:45.500 --> 00:14:47.230
You know, I don't go down
the gym and start boxing

00:14:47.230 --> 00:14:49.270
because of Katie Taylor, but the values,

00:14:49.270 --> 00:14:52.170
and the fundamentals
that she has in terms of

00:14:52.170 --> 00:14:54.270
discipline and drive and winning.

00:14:54.270 --> 00:14:56.250
Would you like your
daughters to be boxers?

00:14:56.250 --> 00:14:57.083
If they wanted to be?

00:14:57.083 --> 00:14:57.916
If they wanted to be.

00:14:57.916 --> 00:14:58.749
You would support that?

00:14:58.749 --> 00:15:01.740
I would. Now, when I talk
about them going into a gym,

00:15:01.740 --> 00:15:04.510
I don't talk about them going
into a gym with a view to

00:15:04.510 --> 00:15:05.570
become a professional fighter.

00:15:05.570 --> 00:15:07.773
No, but it is interesting to
hear sometimes when people

00:15:07.773 --> 00:15:10.390
work in an industry, if they
actually would put their

00:15:10.390 --> 00:15:11.539
most precious person in there.

00:15:11.539 --> 00:15:14.510
I want my, I want my children
to chase their dreams

00:15:14.510 --> 00:15:16.820
in terms of the passion that's, that's,

00:15:16.820 --> 00:15:20.380
that's lit within them, right,
and if that's through boxing,

00:15:20.380 --> 00:15:22.670
then I would one hundred
percent support that. Now,

00:15:22.670 --> 00:15:24.880
if I had a choice, would I
want them to be fighters?

00:15:24.880 --> 00:15:26.810
Absolutely not. If I had a choice,

00:15:26.810 --> 00:15:30.930
would I want them to be part
of our company, my company,

00:15:30.930 --> 00:15:31.763
our family business,

00:15:31.763 --> 00:15:32.920
Like you were with your?

00:15:32.920 --> 00:15:33.753
Not really. No.

00:15:33.753 --> 00:15:35.524
No, not like you took on from your father?

00:15:35.524 --> 00:15:38.360
Not really. Because people
think that it's, you know,

00:15:38.360 --> 00:15:40.440
a nice, you know, or it's
been given the reins,

00:15:40.440 --> 00:15:42.647
if you like, but it's a
different kind of struggle.

00:15:42.647 --> 00:15:44.950
You know, it's not the
struggle to necessarily

00:15:44.950 --> 00:15:47.120
put food on the table, but it's a struggle

00:15:47.120 --> 00:15:50.351
to make your own name in
your own right. Cuz when you,

00:15:50.351 --> 00:15:53.090
when you're born to a successful
father and you see this in

00:15:53.090 --> 00:15:55.640
sport, I mean, Chris Eubank,
you know, with his dad,

00:15:55.640 --> 00:15:58.160
Connor Benn, one of our fighters
with his dad, Nigel Ben,

00:15:58.160 --> 00:16:01.349
you're always Nigel Benn's
son or Chris Eubank's son. So,

00:16:01.349 --> 00:16:03.869
the struggle is more to
create your own identity,

00:16:03.869 --> 00:16:06.020
you know, and to do that, you have to

00:16:06.020 --> 00:16:08.770
go above and beyond what they did.

00:16:08.770 --> 00:16:09.670
Do you feel you've done it?

00:16:09.670 --> 00:16:10.503
Yeah I do.

00:16:10.503 --> 00:16:12.588
What was the moment you felt you did it?

00:16:12.588 --> 00:16:15.078
I think with, with what
we've done for boxing,

00:16:15.078 --> 00:16:17.731
you know, my dad was a
huge promoter in the UK,

00:16:17.731 --> 00:16:18.564
you know, still is of course, snooker

00:16:18.564 --> 00:16:21.700
but never went internationally
and did major TV deals,

00:16:21.700 --> 00:16:24.101
never staged mega fights in America.

00:16:24.101 --> 00:16:26.410
Like I have and all those kind of stuff.

00:16:26.410 --> 00:16:29.020
And that was like a subconscious
chip on the shoulder to

00:16:29.020 --> 00:16:31.737
say that's the only way
I can get respected.

00:16:31.737 --> 00:16:33.870
Because otherwise I've just been given it.

00:16:33.870 --> 00:16:35.290
And I was given it, you
know, I was given an

00:16:35.290 --> 00:16:36.740
opportunity that others wouldn't have.

00:16:36.740 --> 00:16:38.760
So, I had to go above and beyond that,

00:16:38.760 --> 00:16:42.261
but I don't want my daughters
to have the same kind of

00:16:42.261 --> 00:16:45.780
pressures if you like to sort
of try and outperform me,

00:16:45.780 --> 00:16:48.867
but I want them to be
passionate about something.

00:16:48.867 --> 00:16:53.213
And that's why I come back to
sport because we live in an

00:16:53.213 --> 00:16:56.300
age where kids are on their
phones and that you know that

00:16:56.300 --> 00:16:59.310
they're not out, they're not exercising,

00:16:59.310 --> 00:17:02.230
they're reading negativity
on social media.

00:17:02.230 --> 00:17:05.070
And it's just a complete,
you know, influx of,

00:17:05.070 --> 00:17:08.740
of information in that
respect, sport is the answer.

00:17:08.740 --> 00:17:11.840
On your deals and
forging into new markets,

00:17:11.840 --> 00:17:14.490
getting that chip off your
shoulder and doing different,

00:17:14.490 --> 00:17:16.870
doing different deals, bringing
boxing to different places.

00:17:16.870 --> 00:17:19.720
You are forging into new
markets like the Middle East,

00:17:19.720 --> 00:17:22.620
specifically Saudi Arabia.
And you said in 2019,

00:17:22.620 --> 00:17:24.869
you wanted Saudi to be
the home of mega boxing

00:17:24.869 --> 00:17:27.530
taking over from Vegas. How's that going?

00:17:27.530 --> 00:17:29.040
Obviously there's been a global pandemic.

00:17:29.040 --> 00:17:30.949
Yeah, well it was, you know,

00:17:30.949 --> 00:17:33.923
we did Anthony Joshua against
Andy Ruiz, the rematch there,

00:17:33.923 --> 00:17:36.720
which was went very well. You
know, it was a huge event,

00:17:36.720 --> 00:17:40.589
obviously 2020 was
significantly slowed down, 2021,

00:17:40.589 --> 00:17:42.912
sorry, from, from the pandemic,

00:17:42.912 --> 00:17:46.190
there are talks with
not just Saudi Arabia,

00:17:46.190 --> 00:17:47.920
but of course Abu Dhabi, Dubai, and,

00:17:47.920 --> 00:17:50.870
and that region about
bringing major fights there.

00:17:50.870 --> 00:17:53.690
And they're gonna be very
aggressive in that marketplace

00:17:53.690 --> 00:17:56.700
because, you know, there is
a desire from those countries

00:17:56.700 --> 00:17:59.290
to bring major sporting events there.

00:17:59.290 --> 00:18:00.990
You talked though about
trying to do what's right.

00:18:00.990 --> 00:18:03.550
And I wonder, do you have any
moral qualms about a country

00:18:03.550 --> 00:18:06.090
where I don't know, women
weren't allowed to drive

00:18:06.090 --> 00:18:08.660
until recently, human
rights concerns, especially

00:18:08.660 --> 00:18:09.880
with Saudi Arabia?

00:18:09.880 --> 00:18:11.691
Well, I think if they want to change

00:18:11.691 --> 00:18:14.449
and if they're gonna change,
they need to make statements

00:18:14.449 --> 00:18:17.020
and they need to bring a
focus on their territory.

00:18:17.020 --> 00:18:19.450
I mean, you talk about, you
know, 'sportwashing', which is

00:18:19.450 --> 00:18:21.589
a question that really
you're referring to.

00:18:21.589 --> 00:18:22.422
Yeah.

00:18:22.422 --> 00:18:24.701
And the answer is, is if
you want to modernize,

00:18:24.701 --> 00:18:28.540
you know, a, a world or a
country like, like Saudi Arabia,

00:18:28.540 --> 00:18:30.460
they want to do it in many different ways.

00:18:30.460 --> 00:18:33.080
But bringing big events
to that territory is

00:18:33.080 --> 00:18:36.360
very important to them.
When I say about, you know,

00:18:36.360 --> 00:18:40.780
Formula One, going to Saudi
Arabia, the PGA golf tour,

00:18:40.780 --> 00:18:43.833
WWE, major tennis tournaments.

00:18:44.850 --> 00:18:47.838
It's not that I pass
the buck and say, look,

00:18:47.838 --> 00:18:50.153
we're only boxing. What,
what, why shouldn't we?

00:18:51.317 --> 00:18:55.460
But boxing is prize fighting.
Okay. So it's very different.

00:18:55.460 --> 00:18:57.309
I don't really have a right to say to

00:18:57.309 --> 00:19:01.780
Anthony Joshua and Andy
Ruiz, we've had this offer,

00:19:01.780 --> 00:19:04.030
You guys are going in to fight each other.

00:19:04.030 --> 00:19:05.853
And this is the opportunity for you.

00:19:07.031 --> 00:19:09.660
I mean, Andy Murray, of
course, one of our, the UK's,

00:19:09.660 --> 00:19:11.900
greatest tennis exports turned down

00:19:11.900 --> 00:19:13.700
one and a half million
pounds to play in Saudi

00:19:13.700 --> 00:19:15.660
because of human rights
concerns. It does happen.

00:19:15.660 --> 00:19:17.200
I suppose, there are
individuals who take that.

00:19:17.200 --> 00:19:19.059
Yeah, but it was a lot
more money than that, and

00:19:19.059 --> 00:19:21.248
maybe if they offered Andy
Murray more he would have gone

00:19:21.248 --> 00:19:22.787
But the opportunity only seems to be right

00:19:22.787 --> 00:19:23.954
for your fighters if the money is right.

00:19:23.954 --> 00:19:25.430
No. But that was my comment
on Andy Murray. I mean,

00:19:25.430 --> 00:19:26.765
you're not talking about

00:19:26.765 --> 00:19:28.450
No, no, no but you are saying to me,

00:19:28.450 --> 00:19:29.283
they're prize fighters.

00:19:29.283 --> 00:19:30.186
They're prize fighters.

00:19:30.186 --> 00:19:31.737
So I'm trying to get
into the mentality of it.

00:19:31.737 --> 00:19:32.661
But I'll give you an example.

00:19:32.661 --> 00:19:33.494
Yeah, yeah

00:19:33.494 --> 00:19:37.526
If a fighter was offered
five million dollars

00:19:37.526 --> 00:19:39.618
to take a fight and there
was an opportunity to fight

00:19:39.618 --> 00:19:42.016
for thirty-million dollars in a sport,

00:19:42.016 --> 00:19:45.821
which is incredibly
dangerous, and you never know,

00:19:45.821 --> 00:19:49.580
when it's your last opportunity
to be involved in a fight.

00:19:49.580 --> 00:19:52.050
It's a different kind of conversation.

00:19:52.050 --> 00:19:54.870
Rather than a million dollars
to play a tennis match.

00:19:54.870 --> 00:19:57.320
If you Google you, do you do that?

00:19:57.320 --> 00:19:58.519
Do you Google yourself?

00:19:58.519 --> 00:19:59.352
Not,no

00:19:59.352 --> 00:20:00.720
Come on you must a little bit?

00:20:00.720 --> 00:20:02.588
No. Not for a long time, but
I think I probably have done.

00:20:02.588 --> 00:20:03.421
Yeah, for sure.

00:20:03.421 --> 00:20:04.679
Yeah, yeah, You did it last night.

00:20:04.679 --> 00:20:06.424
Eddie Hearn and the memes.

00:20:06.424 --> 00:20:07.324
Oh yeah

00:20:07.324 --> 00:20:09.639
These memes of you. 'No context Hearn'

00:20:09.639 --> 00:20:11.179
is a Twitter account that has

00:20:11.179 --> 00:20:13.920
several hundred thousand
twitter followers.

00:20:13.920 --> 00:20:15.300
What's your favorite one? These are,

00:20:15.300 --> 00:20:17.651
these are things you seem to just do.

00:20:17.651 --> 00:20:18.484
I just, yeah.

00:20:18.484 --> 00:20:19.317
I mean, it's not you, is it?

00:20:19.317 --> 00:20:20.534
Yeah.

00:20:20.534 --> 00:20:21.890
Do you run that account?

00:20:21.890 --> 00:20:22.723
Somebody thought you did.

00:20:22.723 --> 00:20:24.646
Yeah, and it would've
been genius, you know?

00:20:24.646 --> 00:20:26.516
And actually if I'd have thought of it,

00:20:26.516 --> 00:20:27.850
I probably would've started it myself,

00:20:27.850 --> 00:20:29.436
but it's a guy called Andy.

00:20:29.436 --> 00:20:30.737
Oh, you know the person?

00:20:30.737 --> 00:20:32.526
Yeah. He sent me the message on Twitter.

00:20:32.526 --> 00:20:35.788
He works for the NHS and
he said to me, look, I,

00:20:35.788 --> 00:20:38.220
I've watched your interviews.
Would you mind if I do this

00:20:38.220 --> 00:20:41.050
'No Context Hearn'? No context
is something that exists,

00:20:41.050 --> 00:20:43.453
you know, with some other sites as well.

00:20:43.453 --> 00:20:44.286
Yeah.

00:20:44.286 --> 00:20:46.187
And I was like, yeah, crack on mate.

00:20:46.187 --> 00:20:48.669
I mean, how big can that
get? And it just went.

00:20:48.669 --> 00:20:51.295
Two thousand followers, ten
thousand, twenty thousand,

00:20:51.295 --> 00:20:53.981
a hundred thousand, two hundred
thousand, three hundred and

00:20:53.981 --> 00:20:54.814
So he just takes clips of you?

00:20:54.814 --> 00:20:57.000
Clips of my interviews. And then, and then

00:20:57.000 --> 00:21:01.248
people use those clips in
context to something else.

00:21:01.248 --> 00:21:03.240
So it'll say like, you
know, when Liverpool score

00:21:03.240 --> 00:21:05.461
or win away at the weekend
and if you go, yeah.

00:21:05.461 --> 00:21:07.630
So how, how do you feel about that though?

00:21:07.630 --> 00:21:09.270
Cause some people wouldn't like it.

00:21:09.270 --> 00:21:10.630
It's actually worked really well for me.

00:21:10.630 --> 00:21:12.069
They wouldn't like themselves
being out of context.

00:21:12.069 --> 00:21:13.820
I never take myself too seriously, Anyway.

00:21:13.820 --> 00:21:15.080
I think that's very important in life,

00:21:15.080 --> 00:21:18.550
but I actually, it's
worked well for me because

00:21:18.550 --> 00:21:20.520
it's opened up a different
audience. You know,

00:21:20.520 --> 00:21:24.532
I was known by fight fans,
but now you're talking about,

00:21:24.532 --> 00:21:28.202
you know, kids sort of
young adults, you know,

00:21:28.202 --> 00:21:32.983
more women cuz it's everywhere.
And, but the problem is,

00:21:32.983 --> 00:21:35.850
is you get referred to as the meme guy.

00:21:35.850 --> 00:21:38.300
And I took my kids to a
trampoline park the other day

00:21:38.300 --> 00:21:40.500
and there was a girl
letting us in. So I mean,

00:21:40.500 --> 00:21:42.900
she must have been, I don't
know, sixteen, fifteen.

00:21:42.900 --> 00:21:47.440
And she went, is it you? And
I'm thinking, wow, you know,

00:21:47.440 --> 00:21:50.530
Katie Taylor's obviously
working here. A lot of younger.

00:21:50.530 --> 00:21:51.780
She goes, "you're the meme guy".

00:21:51.780 --> 00:21:54.470
And I was like, and my
kids, you know that all,

00:21:54.470 --> 00:21:55.820
when I picked them up from school,

00:21:55.820 --> 00:21:58.360
all the kids are going-
they're not saying that

00:21:58.360 --> 00:22:00.360
because I'm doing a show on Saturday,

00:22:00.360 --> 00:22:02.500
they're doing that because
of 'no context Hearn'. So.

00:22:02.500 --> 00:22:04.652
You're the funny man on the internet.

00:22:04.652 --> 00:22:05.560
Yeah. But you know.

00:22:05.560 --> 00:22:06.690
Have you got a favorite one of you?

00:22:06.690 --> 00:22:08.290
Whatever it takes.

00:22:08.290 --> 00:22:10.130
Is there one of you just
doing something you don't even

00:22:10.130 --> 00:22:11.684
almost remember?

00:22:11.684 --> 00:22:14.057
Yeah. There's, there's so
many. I mean there was me,

00:22:14.057 --> 00:22:16.991
I was selling strawberries
on a, a market store in Hull,

00:22:16.991 --> 00:22:19.580
because one of my boxers, their family,

00:22:19.580 --> 00:22:22.640
has a fruit store in Hull.
A young lady walked past,

00:22:22.640 --> 00:22:23.850
and I said, "you in the headband",

00:22:23.850 --> 00:22:26.000
"you are different gravy" it's just,

00:22:26.000 --> 00:22:28.120
they play it all the time,
and if there's a footballer

00:22:28.120 --> 00:22:30.251
with a headband or I mean, it's just,

00:22:30.251 --> 00:22:31.084
It gets re-used.

00:22:31.084 --> 00:22:33.550
Yeah. And, but there's so many, so many,

00:22:33.550 --> 00:22:35.568
I I've gotta ask you
cuz I thought about this

00:22:35.568 --> 00:22:37.331
and I don't know quite my decision.

00:22:37.331 --> 00:22:41.097
What would be your music
if you were a fighter and

00:22:41.097 --> 00:22:43.710
you were walking in,
what would be the tune

00:22:43.710 --> 00:22:46.110
that you would pick? You
must have imagined yourself

00:22:46.110 --> 00:22:47.411
doing what some of your fighters?

00:22:47.411 --> 00:22:50.000
You know, there's a song
by White Snake called

00:22:50.000 --> 00:22:52.000
'Here I Go Again' or 'Here We Go Again'.

00:22:52.000 --> 00:22:53.033
And it was actually a,

00:22:53.033 --> 00:22:55.480
a song that Micky Ward,
a great Irish fighter

00:22:55.480 --> 00:22:57.770
and American fighter used to walk out to.

00:22:57.770 --> 00:22:59.291
And it's like, yes, yes, here we go again.

00:22:59.291 --> 00:23:01.790
Walking down the only
road I know, it's quite,

00:23:01.790 --> 00:23:02.623
it's quite deep.

00:23:02.623 --> 00:23:04.340
And what would be your style do you think?

00:23:04.340 --> 00:23:06.880
You walk out Eddie Hearn is
there, the man off the internet

00:23:06.880 --> 00:23:09.010
I dunno. I mean just embarrassing.

00:23:09.010 --> 00:23:12.116
Probably like I think I can
dance, I think I can sing.

00:23:12.116 --> 00:23:12.949
Yeah.

00:23:12.949 --> 00:23:15.650
I think I'm very limited
at both, but you know,

00:23:15.650 --> 00:23:17.928
but I think that I would look the part.

00:23:17.928 --> 00:23:18.860
You'd have to.

00:23:18.860 --> 00:23:21.070
Yes. But I think once the bell went,

00:23:21.070 --> 00:23:23.100
it'd be a major problem. Yeah. And it,

00:23:23.100 --> 00:23:25.430
and it would be for me,
not for the other person.

00:23:25.430 --> 00:23:27.710
Eddie Hearn, thank you so
much for talking to me and,

00:23:27.710 --> 00:23:28.543
and to our viewers.

00:23:28.543 --> 00:23:29.376
Thank you.

00:23:29.376 --> 00:23:31.695
And thank you so much for being with us.

00:23:31.695 --> 00:23:35.612
And until we meet again,
stay safe and goodbye.

